1. Export Feature Overview You can create a recurring task that automatically exports your Cost Management data to Azure storage on a daily, or monthly basis. Exports is designed to streamline your FinOps practice by automating the export of other cost-impacting datasets. You can use the exported data with external systems and combine it with your own custom data. 2. Prerequisites To utilize the export functionality, appropriate permissions for both the subscription and storage are required. For subscription: Owner - Can create, modify, or delete scheduled exports for a subscription. Contributor - Can create, modify, or delete their own scheduled exports. Cost Management Contributor - Can view costs and manage cost configurations (e.g., budgets, exports). For Azure Storage accounts: Write permissions are required to change the configured storage account, independent of permissions on the export. Your Azure storage account must be configured for blob or file storage. Don't configure exports to a storage container that is configured as a destination in an object replication rule. To export to storage accounts with firewall rules, you need other privileges on the storage account. These privileges are only required during export creation or modification: Owner role on the storage account or A custom role that includes: Microsoft.Authorization/roleAssignments/write Microsoft.Authorization/permissions/read Common Errors Caused by Permission Issues: If your account only has the Reader or Contributor role for the subscription, you may encounter the following error when accessing a container in the storage account. If this occurs, you can request the owner of the storage account (who is also the owner of the subscription to which the storage account belongs) to assign your account roles such as Storage Blob Data Reader, Storage Blob Data Contributor, or Storage Blob Data Owner. This will allow your account to download the usage report from the container. Roles that may cause errors when creating an export: Reader for subscription Cost Management Reader for subscription 3. Create exports 3.1. Accessing the Export Interface The export interface can be accessed through the following methods. Click Export under Cost Management + Billing on the left sidebar. In Cost Management + Billing, click Cost Analysis on the left sidebar, then select Configure. In Cost Management, click Export or Configure on the left sidebar. 3.2. Select the Billing Scope You can create export jobs at the Billing Account, Billing Profile, Invoice Section, or Subscription level. Supported templates at the Billing Account level: Supported templates at the Billing Profile level: Supported templates at the Invoice Section level: Supported templates at the Subscription level: 3.3. Edit Export Information After selecting the desired export template and providing the relevant information—using Cost and Usage details (Actual) as an example—configure the following: Export Prefix: The name of the export job. The default export job is set to Daily type, which exports data from the beginning of the month to the current date on a daily basis. Click the Edit button to modify these settings. Type of data and the Dataset version If Cost and usage details (actual) and Cost and usage details (amortized) template is selected, the following four dataset versions will be displayed: 2021-10-01, 2021-01-01, 2019-11-01, and 2019-10-01, with 2021-10-01 set as the default. For detailed explanations of these dataset versions, please refer to the documentation: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/dataset-schema/cost-usage-details-mca-partner-subscription#version-2021-10-01 The billing usage report manually downloaded from the Portal is an actual cost bill, which uses the 2021-10-01 dataset—the most detailed dataset version. If the Cost and usage details (FOCUS)** **export template is selected (note that the table contains a large number of header entries — 96 in total — therefore, it is recommended to download the Cost and usage details (actual) ), only three frequency options are available, along with three dataset versions: v1.0, v1.0r2, and v1.0-preview(V1). For detailed information on these dataset versions, please refer to: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/dataset-schema/cost-usage-details-focus Export Frequency, Categorized into the following four types: One-time export (Custom): Custom Date Range. The selected time span must be one month and within the same calendar month. Cross-month selection is not allowed. For example: If you choose an end date of July 2 , but select a start date of June 30 , the system will prompt: “Period must be within the same month.” In this case, you may only select any date range within July. Daily export of month-to-date costs ( Daily ) The export function will generate bills usage report from the 1st of the current month up to the current date at the time of setup. Subsequent exports will occur automatically on a daily basis. For example: If you configure this export on March 10, the system will produce a bill usage report covering the period from March 1 to March 10. The default expiration date is set to February 1, 2050. To stop the automated export, simply click "Disable". Monthly export of last month's costs The export process will generate the previous month's billing usage report on the 5th of the following month. For example: If an export task is created on July 10, the system will produce the July billing usage report on August 5. The default expiration date is set to February 1, 2050. To stop the automated export, click "Disable". Monthly export of last billing month's costs If you create this export job on March 10, it will generate the complete billing usage report for February. The next automated run will occur on the 5th of the following month. Export Description. Custom notes or remarks can be added as needed. 3.4 Define the export destination On the Destination tab, select the Storage type . The default is Azure blob storage. Specify your Azure storage account subscription. Choose an existing resource group or create a new one. Select the Storage account name or create a new one. Container and Directory: Specify the storage container and directory path for the export file. You may enter the container and directory names directly. Format: Choose the Format as CSV or Parquet. Choose the Compression type as None, Gzip for CSV file format, or Snappy for the parquet file format. File partitioning is enabled by default. It splits large files into smaller ones and can't be disabled. Overwrite data is enabled by default. For daily exports, it replaces the previous day's file with an updated file. CSV : Suitable for rapid data exchange, small dataset, or collaboration among non-technical users. Parquet : Preferred for big data analytics and scenarios with frequent reads and infrequent writes. Delta: Ideal for applications requiring transaction support, frequent updates, or building Data Lake, often integrated with the Spark ecosystem. If further optimization of cloud storage costs (combined with FinOps) is needed, the high compression rates of Parquet and Delta are advantageous, while Delta’s version control feature helps reduce cost waste caused by data errors. 3.5 Review and create Review your export configuration and make any necessary changes. When done, select Review + create to complete the process. Your new export appears in the list of exports. By default, new exports are enabled. If you want to disable or delete a scheduled export, select any item in the list, and then select either Disable or Delete. The export process can take up to 24 hours to complete before the data is ready. 4. Manage exports You can view the list of available exports by navigating to the Exports page and manage individual exports by selecting them. You can perform the following actions on individual exports. Run now - Queues an unplanned export to run at the next available moment, regardless of the scheduled run time. Export selected dates - Reruns an export for a historical date range instead of creating a new one-time export. You can extract up to 13 months of historical data in one-month chunks. This option isn't available for price sheets. Disable - Temporarily suspends the export job. Delete - Permanently removes the export. Refresh - Updates the Run history. 5. Download usage report In the export list, select the storage account name. Navigate to the container you wish to access and select the folder corresponding to the current month. Click "Download" on the following page. 6. FAQ Below are some frequently asked questions and answers regarding exports. 1）Dataset Versions and Data fieldsExplanations: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/dataset-schema/schema-index 2） The currently supported export scopes include: Billing Account level, Billing Profile level, Invoice Section level, Individual Subscription level, Exports at the Management Group scope are currently not supported. Attempting to create an export job for a management group will result 3） error: 4） To export to a storage account with firewall rules configured, your account requires additional permissions. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-improved-exports Error Message: { "error":{

"code":"Unauthorized",

"message":"The user does not have authorization to perform 'Microsoft.Authorization/roleAssignments/write' action on specified storage account, please use a storage account with sufficient permissions. If the permissions have changed recently then retry after some time."

} } 5） Why and when file partitioning is applied To improve reliability and scalability, Cost Management exports automatically partition large files into smaller chunks. Partitioning helps address challenges with downloading or opening large single files, especially over unreliable networks or in tools with file size or row count limitations, such as Microsoft Excel. In exports experience, partitioning is always enabled. Files are split based on size—not row count—with each uncompressed file kept under 1 GB. For compressed formats like Gzip, actual file sizes may vary depending on compression efficiency. Partitioning is applied consistently, even for small exports. This ensures compatibility with downstream systems, supports enterprise-scale automation, and avoids inconsistencies or failures in reporting workflows. 6） What costs might be involved when using the export feature? When exporting multiple types of cost management datasets to Azure Storage, you will incur storage costs and network charges. For more information, please refer to the documentation: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-improved-exports

